WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:01.939
Task statement 3.4.

2
00:00:01.939 --> 00:00:05.879
Determine when to use plan mode vs direct execution.

3
00:00:05.879 --> 00:00:14.516
Rewriting auth across 30 services without a plan means expensive rework; planning a one-line typo fix wastes time.

4
00:00:15.916 --> 00:00:18.062
Claude Code can work in two styles.

5
00:00:18.062 --> 00:00:24.256
In plan mode it explores and writes a proposal, but leaves your source files alone until you approve.

6
00:00:24.256 --> 00:00:27.139
In direct execution it simply makes the change.

7
00:00:27.139 --> 00:00:32.597
You would not draw blueprints to oil a hinge, and you would not move a wall without them.

8
00:00:32.597 --> 00:00:36.460
The exam tests whether you can tell which situation you are in.

9
00:00:37.860 --> 00:00:46.913
Use plan mode when the change spans many files, affects architecture, touches code you do not know well, or has more than one reasonable approach.

10
00:00:46.913 --> 00:00:50.944
Use direct execution when the change is small and clearly scoped.

11
00:00:50.944 --> 00:00:56.401
A handy test from the docs: if you can describe the diff in one sentence, skip the plan.

12
00:00:56.401 --> 00:00:59.129
After a plan is agreed, execute it directly.

13
00:01:00.529 --> 00:01:04.200
Before planning, Claude often needs to understand the code.

14
00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:10.173
Reading dozens of files in the main conversation fills it with detail you will never need again.

15
00:01:10.173 --> 00:01:19.879
The built in Explore subagent does that discovery in its own context and returns a summary, so your main session stays focused on the decision and the plan.

16
00:01:21.279 --> 00:01:26.528
In the code generation scenario, the same team gets three requests in a morning.

17
00:01:26.528 --> 00:01:30.662
The cross service library swap needs a plan everyone agrees on.

18
00:01:30.662 --> 00:01:34.731
The null check with a clear stack trace is a quick direct fix.

19
00:01:34.731 --> 00:01:42.015
The legacy module is unknown territory, so Claude explores it first with a subagent, then plans, then executes.

20
00:01:43.415 --> 00:01:47.411
Both modes are good tools, so the trap is using the wrong one.

21
00:01:47.411 --> 00:01:49.474
Planning a trivial fix is waste.

22
00:01:49.474 --> 00:01:53.213
Diving straight into an architectural change risks rework.

23
00:01:53.213 --> 00:02:01.207
And if the question mentions the main context filling up during discovery, the answer usually involves the Explore subagent.

24
00:02:02.607 --> 00:02:04.415
Match the mode to the task.

25
00:02:04.415 --> 00:02:07.362
Big, uncertain, multi file work gets a plan.

26
00:02:07.362 --> 00:02:09.974
Small, clear changes get done directly.

27
00:02:09.974 --> 00:02:14.595
Discovery that would flood your context goes to the Explore subagent.

28
00:02:14.595 --> 00:02:18.948
And once the plan is approved, switch to execution and follow it.

